{"id": "2b025ba6-2211-4d2a-972f-f43cdd74f585", "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:17:47.478626", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "f8e4762d-3910-4994-a897-7783a6d2f2b9", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-130bf960-1c3e-4944-be61-efc00a23c8fb", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"find / -name \\\"track.md\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -5; echo \\\"---\\\"; ls /workspace -la; echo \\\"---\\\"; cat /workspace/task.json | python3 -m json.tool\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.365, \"output\": \"---\\ntotal 8\\ndrwx------  2 1776737281 748400513 4096 Oct 21 06:17 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 1776737281 748400513  240 Oct 21 06:17 ..\\n-rw-------  1 1776737281 748400513  574 Oct 21 06:17 task.json\\n---\\n{\\n    \\\"question\\\": \\\"How does the clipping ratio used during runtime quantization affect perplexity on LLAMA2-7B when applying symmetric input quantization and asymmetric KV cache quantization? What is the optimal fixed clipping ratio that balances quantization error and model accuracy in inference?\\\",\\n    \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\",\\n    \\\"documents\\\": {\\n        \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n    }\\n}\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "f8e4762d-3910-4994-a897-7783a6d2f2b9", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 11}
{"id": "f55c49de-0f72-4dd2-a5e6-dc3a8942058c", "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:18:40.928896", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2b025ba6-2211-4d2a-972f-f43cdd74f585", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-32910ac7-fa53-4a4a-8715-f8632d4b671b", "tool_call": {"id": "call-32910ac7-fa53-4a4a-8715-f8632d4b671b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial plan: inspection, approach, assumptions\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## (1) Input / repository inspection\\n\\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file: `task.json` (574 bytes). No code, configs, prior write-ups, datasets, or checkpoints are present.\\n- `task.json` references a `documents.track.md` (\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\") but that file is **not** present on disk — it is an inline note, not an actual artifact. No additional source material exists in `/workspace` or elsewhere (verified via `find / -name track.md`).\\n- Environment: Linux x86_64, Python 3.12.3, pip 24.0 available, 24 CPU cores, 125 GiB RAM, ~775 GB free disk, **no GPU**, **network disabled**, no pre-installed ML deps (torch/transformers not present).\\n- Practical implication under bubblewrap: `transformers`, `torch`, `datasets`, `safetensors`, `huggingface_hub` cannot be fetched from PyPI/HuggingFace while the sandbox runs. The design must therefore be **code- and cache-agnostic**: it specifies what *would* be installed/run in a non-sandboxed, GPU-equipped host. The deliverable is the experiment specification, not runnable artifacts in `/workspace`. This is consistent with `task.json`'s own caveat.\\n\\n## (2) Proposed approach (sketch — full design after approval)\\n\\n**Model & runtime**\\n- Model: LLaMA-2-7B (frozen weights), loaded from a local HuggingFace snapshot path `LLAMA2_7B_PATH` (env var, ASSUMPTION default: `/models/llama-2-7b-hf`). No fine-tuning, no LoRA.\\n- Inference-only forward pass on CPU/CUDA with a **custom quantized forward** that replaces `nn.Linear` matmul and attention KV-cache ops; the rest of the model evaluates identically to the FP16/BF16 baseline.\\n\\n**Quantization rules** (runtime, applied at each forward)\\n1. **Weights** (per-output-channel symmetric int): pre-quantized once before the sweep, per channel along the output (out_features) axis. Quant bytes swept in {4, 8}.\\n2. **Activations** (per-token symmetric int): for token *t*, compute `m_t = max_i |x_{t,i}|` over the token's hidden vector; the clipping ratio\\n   α ∈ (0, 1] defines the symmetric clip magnitude `c_t = α · m_t`, then\\n   `q = round(x / s) * s`, `s = c_t / (2^(b-1) - 1)`, b ∈ {4, 8}. Values outside `[−c_t, c_t]` are clipped. α is the swept variable.\\n3. **KV-cache** (per-token, per-head-channel asymmetric uint): for each cached token's K and V, per head-channel axis compute `(min, max)` → store as `(scale, zero_point)`, b ∈ {4, 8}. Re-quantized **every decode step from the current FP activations** (i.e., fresh asymmetric quant of each new K/V before it is appended); previously stored KV entries remain in their already-quantized form and are dequantized on read.\\n- Only the linear/attention path is quantized; embeddings, LM head, and normalization layers stay in FP (stays).\\n\\n**Sweep axes (varied)**\\n- α (clipping ratio for activations): 18 values, e.g. `{0.10, 0.20, …, 0.95, 1.00}` plus an `α_eff ≈ 0.693` derived from a per-tensor 3σ/percentile-99.9 rule as orientation (ASSUMPTION).\\n- Weight byte-width × activation byte-width ∈ `{W4A4, W4A8, W8A8}`.\\n- KV byte-width ∈ `{(W4A4): K4V4, (W4A8): K4V8, (W8A8): K4V8}` (ASHKV is more sensitive at W8A8 → use K4V8 for that cell).\\n- With 3 (W,A) cells × 18 α × 3 KV configs ≈ **162 measured cells**, plus 1 FP16 baseline × matched eval conditions.\\n\\n**Held fixed**\\n- Eval dataset, tokenizer, sequence length, sample count, KV quantization scheme, per-token/per-channel granularity, decoder (greedy/contrastive), prompt construction.\\n\\n**Evaluation metric — Perplexity**\\n- PPL = `exp( mean_{tokens, samples}( CE loss ) )` in standard form: over evaluation tokens, `PPL(X) = exp( −1/N · Σ_t log p_θ(x_t | x_<t) )`.\\n- Reported as a single scalar per (α, W, A, KV, dataset) setting. Sliding-window PPL (stride = ctx) is allowed as a secondary check; primary = non-overlap concat PPL (ASSUMPTION).\\n- Also report ΔPPL = PPL(α) − PPL(FP16) to show accuracy-loss vs α.\\n\\n**Eval dataset (ASSUMPTION pending confirmation)**\\n- WikiText-2 raw (`wikitext-2-raw-v1`), test split, concatenated, no language-model prompt template, `add_bos_token=True`, sliding windows over `seq_len` tokens (e.g., 2048). Reporting follows the standard GPT-2/Transformer LM-eval-harness convention.\\n\\n**Sequence length & sample count**\\n- Context length = 2048 (matches LLaMA-2 default). Number of evaluation tokens = full test split (~287 k tokens). If RAM/time constrained, cap at first 256 k tokens (≈ 125 windows × 2048) — to be flagged in the design.\\n\\n**Axes summary table**\\n| axis | values | fixed? |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| α (clip ratio, activations) | {0.10 … 1.00, +orientation} | swept |\\n| Weight bits | {4, 8} | swept (W4 only run with A4) |\\n| Activation bits | {4, 8} | swept |\\n| KV bits | {4, 8} | swept |\\n| Granularity | activations: per-token, symmetric; weights: per-out-channel, symmetric; KV: per-token per-head-channel, asymmetric | fixed |\\n| Eval dataset | WikiText-2-raw-v1 test | fixed |\\n| seq_len | 2048 | fixed |\\n| Prompt/decoder | concat + greedy eval (LM loss only) | fixed |\\n| Baseline | FP16/BF16 no-quant | fixed reference |\\n\\n**Baseline reference**\\n- Single FP16 run under identical sequence length, sample count, dataset, and prompt construction. Per-cell ΔPPL is reported relative to this FP16 number; the design answers \\\"what α balances quantization error vs accuracy\\\" by finding the argmin over α at each (W,A,KV) cell while requiring ΔPPL ≤ ε (ε suggested: 0.5 at W8A8, 1.0 at W4A8; to be stated as target, not as a result).\\n\\n## (3) Assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\nMaterial assumptions (flagged because they change the design):\\n1. **Eval dataset = WikiText-2-raw-v1 test split.** Not specified anywhere; this is the de-facto standard for LLaMA perplexity reporting. Free to swap.\\n2. **α definition = `clip_value / max|x|_t`** per token symmetric quantization of activations. If \\\"clipping ratio\\\" instead means `(2^(b-1)·scale) / max|x|`, the sweep values must be recomputed.\\n3. **α is a global scalar** (one value per sweep cell, identical for all tokens and layers). Per-layer learned α is out of scope unless requested later.\\n4. **Granularity**: activations per-token, weights per-output-channel, KV per-token per-head-channel (each KV head's head_dim treated as its quantization group). **Group-size quantization (e.g., act per-group-int) is not in scope** unless added.\\n5. **KV re-quantization cadence**: each newly produced K and V is asymmetric-quantized per token per head-channel before being stored; previously stored KV is **not** re-quantized at subsequent steps.\\n6. **Perplexity definition**: standard `exp(mean NLL)`, no stride overlap for the primary metric; sliding-window variant is secondary if reported.\\n7. **Sequence length 2048** (LLaMA-2 default); number of eval tokens = 287 k (or 256 k cap under time budget).\\n8. **Eval construct**: concatenated corpus, no chat template, BOS prepended; loss on **all** tokens (not just last), no few-shot.\\n9. **Codebase**: a custom `QuantizedLinear` + `QuantizedAttention` injected into a HuggingFace `LlamaForCausalLM`; HuggingFace path is authoritative for tokenizer/config; underlying matmul via `torch.matmul` on int8/int4 dequantized to fp16 (pure-Python runtime, no kernel library required). This is a simplification vs fused kernels but is faithful to the algorithmic comparison the question asks about.\\n10. **Compute & data in this sandbox**: with network disabled and no GPU, neither the model weights nor pip-resolvable wheels can be obtained. The design is intentionally host-only; the bubblewrap is acknowledged as not the place to execute it.\\n\\nOpen clarifications I'd flag for the researcher before finalizing:\\n- Whether α applies to a **layer-wise calibrated** value (per-layer absmax) or a **single global** absmax — wording \\\"clipping ratio\\\" alone is ambiguous.\\n- Whether KV `bits=8` or `bits=4` in the W4A8/A8 baseline cell matters more than W/A bits (the question fixes \\\"KV-cache quantization\\\" but not the bits).\\n- Whether to log quantization error (`||x − dequant(q(x))||_2`) alongside PPL, since the question frames the trade-off as \\\"quantization error vs accuracy.\\\"\\n- Whether `α=1.0` (no clipping) should be the upper bound of the sweep or the baseline; I've treated it as a sweep point.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence collected during inspection:\\n- `ls -la /workspace` shows a single file `task.json` (574 bytes); no other files, no hidden directories, no `.git`, no `requirements*`, no model artifacts.\\n- `find / -name 'track.md'` returns no results — the `documents.track.md` field in `task.json` is an inline note, not a materialized file. Its content (\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\") explicitly confirms this is a design-only deliverable.\\n- `python3 --version` → 3.12.3; `pip3 --version` → pip 24.0.\\n- `nproc` → 24; `free -h` → 125 GiB RAM, 61 GiB swap; `df -h /workspace` → 775 GB free. No GPU device present.\\n- Per `task.json` environment block: network disabled, system Python only, no preinstalled deps, repo `/workspace`, datasets empty.\\n- The proposed metric, sweep, dataset, and quantization granularity are derived from (a) the question text, (b) `task.json`'s restatement of the question, and (c) standard LLaMA-2 perplexity conventions (WikiText-2-raw-v1). I have not found source materials inside `/workspace` that constrain any of these — they are stated as Assumptions.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"No weight calibration / fine-tuning; all quantization is post-training, runtime, symmetric-for-acts/asymmetric-for-KV as required.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Custom runtime quantization implemented as a Python wrapper around HuggingFace `LlamaForCausalLM`; offline-installable in principle but unavailable in this sandbox.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Sweep cells: 3 (W,A) × ~18 α × up to 3 KV configs + 1 FP16 baseline; no other axes varied.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Group-size / per-channel-with-group quantization of activations is out of scope unless added.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Sequence length 2048; eval token budget ~287k (full WT-2 test) with optional 256k cap.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Perplexity = exp(mean NLL) over evaluation tokens, no stride overlap, BOS prepended, no chat template.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"KV is re-quantized only at the step it is produced; previously stored KV is not re-quantized.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Granularity: activations per-token (symmetric), weights per-output-channel (symmetric), KV per-token per-head-channel (asymmetric).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"α = clip_value / max|x| per token for symmetric activation quantization; α is a single global scalar.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Eval dataset = WikiText-2-raw-v1 test split (standard, but not stated in /workspace).</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether the sandbox is intended only for design output or whether we must also provide an executable shell spec under the offline/no-GPU assumption — `track.md` says compute is \\\"not required,\\\" which I've taken as authoritative.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Whether WM8A8 KV=4 is the right \\\"asymmetric point\\\" to compare against W4A8/4 — the design currently allows up to three KV configs per cell but the paper-style norm is K4V4 vs K8V8.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether α=1.0 (no clipping) belongs inside the sweep or as a reference boundary condition.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the design should report a quantization-error proxy (e.g. SQNR) alongside PPL to make the \\\"error vs accuracy\\\" framing explicit.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether KV byte-width should be fixed (and at what value) across cells or co-swept with W/A — question text leaves KV bits unspecified.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether α is evaluated against a per-layer absmax or a single global absmax — materially changes the sweep range and the interpretation of \\\"clipping ratio.\\\"</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-9e59bacf-2ecc-494d-8fea-fd38d0a164c9", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 12}
