{"id": "fba18cc2-fd92-4654-b0cf-2f19a2228259", "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:22:28.370704", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "0213b52f-8d1a-478f-a4c3-073ad6ccaa3b", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design — Effect of Clipping Ratio α on Perplexity under Runtime Symmetric-Input / Asymmetric-KV Quantization (LLaMA-2-7B)\n\n> **Status:** Design-only specification. No code is executed, no measurements fabricated, no outcome claimed. This document is the deliverable.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Title\n\n**Sensitivity of LLaMA-2-7B Perplexity to the Activation Clipping Ratio α under Symmetric Per-Token Input Quantization and Asymmetric Per-Token Per-Head-Channel KV-Cache Quantization.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Research question & hypotheses\n\n### 2.1 Research question\n*For LLaMA-2-7B, holding the quantization scheme (W: per-out-channel symmetric, A: per-token symmetric, KV: per-token per-head-channel asymmetric) and bit widths in {W4A4, W4A8, W8A8} × {K4V4, K4V8, K8V8} fixed, how does the global activation clipping ratio α ∈ (0, 1] affect end-to-end perplexity on WikiText-2-raw-v1, and what value of α minimizes perplexity per (W,A,KV) cell?*\n\n### 2.2 Hypotheses (predictions, not results)\nH1 (U-shape). *For every non-degenerate (W,A,KV) cell, perplexity as a function of α is U-shaped:* high at α → 0 (severe clipping → large distortion from deleted activations), decreasing toward a minimum at some α\\* ∈ (0.5, 1.0), then mildly increasing back as α → 1.0 (no clipping → scale s_t is too coarse relative to the bulk of the distribution, so low α already known α\\* ≈ 0.7–0.9 from prior literature on symmetrical activation clipping.*\n\nH2 (Bits dependence). *The location of α\\* moves closer to 1.0 as activation bits decrease* — at A4 the optimal α is lower than at A8 because aggressive quant bins reward smaller dynamic range; at A8 the cost of clipping outweighs the quantization-step benefit sooner, so α\\* tends higher.\n\nH3 (KV is mostly orthogonal to α\\*). *α\\* is determined primarily by activation statistics and is approximately invariant to KV bits once KV is asymmetric per-token per-head-channel; KV mainly shifts the curve vertically (ΔPPL floor) rather than horizontally (α\\*).*\n\nH4 (SQNR peak co-locates with PPL minimum). *Per-token activation SQNR (dB) is maximized roughly at α\\* because SQNR captures the trade-off between clipping error and quantization-step error; matching peaks at α\\* support the U-shape inference.*\n\n---\n\n## 3. Prerequisites & environment\n\n### 3.1 Hardware\n- **Primary target:** 1× NVIDIA A100-80GB (compute capability 8.0). 24 CPU cores available for data loading; KV flows through GPU.\n- **Memory budget:** ~14 GB for FP16 weights of LLaMA-2-7B; with quantized weights pre-cast it fits comfortably; KV cache × (seq_len, n_layers, n_heads, head_dim) at W4A8/W8A8 fits within ~16 GB for seq_len = 2048.\n- **Disk:** ≥ 30 GB free for weights snapshot + datasets cache.\n\n### 3.2 Software (host environment, not the bubblewrap)\n- Python 3.10+; `torch ≥ 2.1` (CUDA build); `transformers ≥ 4.40`; `datasets ≥ 2.18`; `huggingface_hub`; `safetensors`; `numpy`; `pandas`; `pyarrow`.\n- Inference path: HuggingFace `LlamaForCausalLM` with a custom **monkey-patch** layer that replaces `nn.Linear` forward and attention KV-store with the quantized runtime described in §5; HF remains the source of truth for tokenizer, RoPE, and config.\n\n### 3.3 Sandbox caveat (acknowledged, not a blocker)\nThe current `/workspace` is a bubblewrap without GPU and without network, so neither weights nor pip dependencies can be obtained here. The deliverable is **the specification of what runs on the A100 host**; the sandbox is used only to inspect and author the design (consistent with the inline note in `task.json`).\n\n### 3.4 Seed & determinism\n- `torch.manual_seed(0)`, `torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(0)`; cuDNN disabled (deterministic path for evaluation). Greedy eval (no sampling); LM-loss only. HF `transformers` eval utilities compute the same loss on the same input regardless of seed, so seed primarily affects any random tie-breaking in tie-breaker passes; results are reproducible up to BF16 vs FP16 numerics on the host.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Model and dataset specifics\n\n### 4.1 Model — LLaMA-2-7B\n- HuggingFace snapshot path supplied via env var `LLAMA2_7B_PATH` (ASSUMPTION default `/models/llama-2-7b-hf`).\n- Loaded as `AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(LLAMA2_7B_PATH, torch_dtype=torch.float16)` (FP16 baseline reference) and `(…, torch_dtype=torch.float16)` with the patched quantized forward for swept cells.\n- Tokenizer: `AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(LLAMA2_7B_PATH)`, `use_fast=True`, `bos_token` prepended on the full concatenated string before windowing (single global BOS).\n- **Frozen weights**, no fine-tuning, no LoRA, no PEFT, no quantization-aware training.\n\n### 4.2 Dataset — WikiText-2 raw (ASSUMPTION A2)\n- HuggingFace `datasets.load_dataset(\"wikitext\", \"wikitext-2-raw-v1\", split=\"test\")`.\n- Concatenate the test split into a single string with the HF-eval-harness convention: rows joined by `\\n\\n` so paragraph breaks are preserved. Append EOS between documents via `tokenizer.eos_token`.\n- No chat template; no few-shot prompts; no system message.\n\n### 4.3 Tokenization & windowing\n- Concatenate, tokenize once → a single int64 vector of length N ≈ 287 247 tokens (full WT-2 raw test split).\n- **Primary PPL (§8.1):** N // `seq_len` non-overlapping windows of exactly `seq_len = 2048` tokens; full-window language-model loss accumulated.\n- **Secondary PPL (§8.2):** sliding windows of `seq_len = 2048` with stride 2048 (i.e. same boundary, included only as a robustness cross-check; equivalent to primary when N is a multiple of seq_len).\n\n### 4.4 Sequence length and sample count\n- `seq_len = 2048` (A3). \n- Number of evaluation windows: N // 2048; number of evaluation tokens N_primary = (N // 2048) × 2048.\n- The clipped tail of N (N − N_primary tokens) is **discarded** for primary PPL; it stays discarded for all cells to keep the comparison fair.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Quantization formulation\n\n### 5.1 Notation\n- Weight tensor `W ∈ ℝ^{d_out × d_in}`; activation row `x_t ∈ ℝ^{1 × d_in}` (one token's pre-matmul input); KV head-row `k_{t,h}, v_{t,h} ∈ ℝ^{1 × head_dim}`.\n- Bit widths: `b_w`, `b_a`, `b_k`, `b_v` ∈ {4, 8}. Integer levels: int8 range `[−Q_a, Q_a]` with `Q_a = 2^(b_a − 1) − 1`; uint range `[0, Q_u]` with `Q_u = 2^b_a − 1`.\n- Clip ratio: scalar α ∈ (0, 1], single global value (A1).\n\n### 5.2 W — Per-output-channel symmetric integer (pre-quantized)\nFor each output channel `c ∈ {1,…,d_out}`:\n```\nm_w[c]   = max_i  |W[c, i]|\ns_w[c]   = m_w[c] / Q_a_w        (Q_a_w = 2^(b_w − 1) − 1)\nW_q[c,i] = clamp(round(W[c,i] / s_w[c]), −Q_a_w, Q_a_w)\nW_dequant[c,i] = W_q[c,i] · s_w[c]\n```\n- Quantization is performed **once before the sweep**, stored as `W_q (int8/int32)` and `s_w (fp16)`.\n\n### 5.3 A — Per-token symmetric integer (runtime, swept α)\nFor each token row `x_t` entering a `nn.Linear`:\n```\nm_t    = max_i |x_t[i]|\nc_t    = α · m_t                              # α=1.0 ⇒ c_t=m_t (no clipping boundary)\ns_t    = c_t / Q_a_a                          # Q_a_a = 2^(b_a − 1) − 1\nx_clipped[t,i] = clamp(x_t[i], −c_t, +c_t)\nx_q[t,i] = round(x_clipped[t,i] / s_t)\nx_dequant[t,i] = x_q[t,i] · s_t              # used inside the matmul\n```\n- Applied **on the fly**; pseudo-static for stable tensors (e.g. attention inputs after residual add are treated as fresh tokens because the per-token max depends on the running sequence).\n\n### 5.4 KV — Per-token per-head-channel asymmetric unsigned (runtime)\nFor each token *t*, for each head *h*, the head-row is a contiguous slice of length `head_dim`:\n```\nk_min   = min_i k_{t,h}[i]\nk_max   = max_i k_{t,h}[i]\ns_k     = (k_max − k_min) / max(Q_u_k, 1)    # Q_u_k = 2^b_k − 1\nz_k     = round(−k_min / s_k)                # zero-point, integer\nk_q[i]  = clamp(round(k_{t,h}[i] / s_k) + z_k, 0, Q_u_k)\nk_dequant[i] = (k_q[i] − z_k) · s_k\n```\n- Same formula for V → `s_v`, `z_v`.\n- Quantization is performed **per newly produced token** (re-quantization cadence per token, A7); previously stored KV entries remain in their quantized form and are dequantized when read by attention.\n\n### 5.5 Pseudo-code for the patched quantized forward (single transformer block)\n\n```\nforward_one_block(x_t, layer_state, α, b_w, b_a, b_k, b_v):\n    # x_t: (B, T, D), T tokens, B=1 in eval\n\n    # --- Attention block ---\n    # Norm + W_q, W_k, W_v matmuls → use W_q + runtime act quant (A)\n    x_norm = RMSNorm(x_t)\n    q_per_tok, s_a_q = quant_act_per_token(x_norm @ W_q^T, b_a, α)\n    k_per_tok, s_a_k = quant_act_per_token(x_norm @ W_k^T, b_a, α)\n    v_per_tok, s_a_v = quant_act_per_token(x_norm @ W_v^T, b_a, α)\n\n    # Re-quantize K and V to asymmetric uint KV-grid for cache storage\n    K_signed = k_per_tok * s_a_k                      # dequant back to FP\n    V_signed = v_per_tok * s_a_v\n    for h in n_heads:\n        for t in T_newly_added:\n            k_q, s_k, z_k, m_k_w = quant_kv_per_token_per_head(K_signed[t, h, :], b_k)\n            v_q, s_v, z_v, m_v_w = quant_kv_per_token_per_head(V_signed[t, h, :], b_v)\n            layer_state.cache_k.append(k_q, s_k, z_k)\n            layer_state.cache_v.append(v_q, s_v, z_v)\n\n    # Attention: Q stays FP16 (or per-token int), K/V read → dequant to FP16\n    K_full = dequant_kv_cache(layer_state.cache_k)     # (B, T_total, n_heads, head_dim)\n    V_full = dequant_kv_cache(layer_state.cache_v)\n    attn_out = scaled_dot_product_attention(q_per_tok, K_full, V_full)\n\n    # --- Output projection ---\n    attn_norm = RMSNorm(attn_out + x_t)                # residual outside quant scope\n    a_in_q, s_a_o = quant_act_per_token(attn_norm, b_a, α)\n    out = (a_in_q * s_a_o) @ W_o_dequant              # W_o is per-out-channel symmetric\n\n    # --- FFN ---\n    h1 = (quant_act_per_token(RMSNorm(out), b_a, α)) @ W_1_dequant\n    h1_act = swiglu(h1)\n    h2 = (quant_act_per_token(h1_act, b_a, α)) @ W_2_dequant\n    return x_t + out + h2, layer_state\n```\n\nNumbers logged per block (to compute SQNR, §9):\n- `m_c_t_w = max_t (max_i |W_w[i]| / s_w[c] − dequant(W_q))` (weight quantization noise)\n- Residual quantization noise `e_a[t] = x_t − dequant(quant(x_t))` per token\n- KV quantization noise `e_k`, `e_v` per token per head.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Sweep design\n\n### 6.1 Sweep axes\n\n| axis | values | role |\n|---|---|---|\n| α (clip ratio, activations) | `{0.10, 0.20, 0.30, …, 0.95, 1.00}` (18 points) + orientation `α ≈ 0.693` (mean-field 3σ for a Gaussian) | primary swept axis |\n| Weight bits `b_w` | {4, 8} | swept |\n| Activation bits `b_a` | {4, 8} | swept (constraint `b_a ≥ 4`; b_w=4 only combined with b_a=4) |\n| KV bits `b_k` × `b_v` | {K4V4, K4V8, K8V8} | swept per (b_w, b_a) cell |\n| Activation granularity | per-token, symmetric | fixed |\n| Weight granularity | per-output-channel, symmetric | fixed |\n| KV granularity | per-token per-head-channel, asymmetric | fixed |\n| Eval dataset | WikiText-2-raw-v1 test | fixed |\n| `seq_len` | 2048 | fixed |\n| Prompt construct | concat + BOS, no chat template, LM-loss on all tokens | fixed |\n| Baseline | FP16, no quant | fixed reference |\n| α granularity | single global scalar across layers (A1) | fixed |\n\n### 6.2 (W,A) cells\nEffective cells (per researcher constraint): `{W4A4, W4A8, W8A8}` — 3 cells.\n\n### 6.3 Cell count — full sweep\nPer cell: 19 α × 3 KV configs = 57 measured runs.\nAcross cells: 3 × 57 = **171 measured runs**, plus:\n- 1 × FP16 baseline\n- Control ablation α=1.0 (boundary reference) — already in the 171; reused\n- Control ablation α→0 — already in the 171 (use α = 0.10)\n- Sensitivity variant S1 (per-layer α instead of global): 1 representative α\\*-region value × 3 cells × 3 KV = 9 runs\n- Sensitivity variant S2 (per-tensor activations vs per-token): 1 representative α-value × 3 cells × 3 KV = 9 runs\n- Per-layer-α sweep shortcut (if time): 3 cells × 3 KV × 3 α = 27 runs\n\n**Total target ≈ 171 + 1 + 9 + 9 = 190 runs** (without shortcut).\n\n### 6.4 Reduced-mode fallback (when compute is tight)\n- Reduce α grid to 6 evenly spaced values: `{0.30, 0.45, 0.60, 0.75, 0.90, 1.00}`.\n- Reduced cell count: 3 × 6 × 3 = **54 measured runs**, plus 1 FP16 + 9 + 9 = **73 runs**.\n- Mandatory anchor: always include α ∈ {0.30, 0.45, 0.60, 0.75, 0.90, 1.00} and at least K4V4 in every cell so the U-shape can be verified.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Controls\n\n| control | purpose | details |\n|---|---|---|\n| C0 — FP16 baseline | reference for ΔPPL | identical tokenizer, prompt, seq_len, eval dataset |\n| C1 — α = 1.0 (boundary) | shows effect of disabling clipping | α = 1.00 is itself a sweep point; reinforces H1 |\n| C2 — α → 0 (extreme clipping) | shows failure mode | α = 0.10; PPL expected to explode, anchors the U-shape's left arm |\n| C3 — per-layer α (sensitivity) | test whether global α misses a better profile | run with `(α_l)` per-layer grid derived from absmax calibration (1 epoch) |\n| C4 — per-tensor activations (granularity sensitivity) | test whether per-token assumption is load-bearing | quantize per-tensor symmetric for activations only; everything else unchanged; representative run at one mid-α per cell |\n| C5 — K8V8 vs K4V8 (asymmetric sensitivity) | check whether asymmetric K/V width matters more than α | included automatically by sweeping (b_k, b_v) |\n| C6 — KV-bits-fixed variants | test orthogonality hypothesis (H3) | the full sweep already covers; no extra run |\n| C7 — orientation α ≈ 0.693 (Gaussian 3σ rule) | predictor vs measurement check | 1 per (cell, KV) = 9 runs |\n| C8 — duplicated FP16 baseline at sweep midpoint | numerical drift check | re-run FP16 every ~30 cells to flag BF16/FP16 drift |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Perplexity metric definition\n\n### 8.1 Primary — Concatenated non-overlapping PPL\n- Build a concatenated int64 token stream of length N.\n- Compute P(N_primary // seq_len) windows of length seq_len = 2048, no overlap; the first seq_len tokens feed each window.\n- For window `w`, HF-style shifted LM loss: `ℓ_w = mean_{t∈[1, seq_len)}  −log p_θ(x_w[t+1] | x_w[≤t])`.\n- **Primary:**\n  `PPL_primary = exp( (Σ_w Σ_t ℓ_w) / N_primary )`.\n\n### 8.2 Secondary — Sliding-window PPL (stride = seq_len)\n- Same non-overlapping partition as primary (since stride = seq_len), but computed as `PPL_windowed = exp( mean_w ℓ_w )`.\n- Acts as an arithmetic-mean variant to guard against pathological window-weighting in §8.1.\n\n### 8.3 ΔPPL (always reported per cell)\n`ΔPPL(α; W,A,KV) = PPL_primary(α; W,A,KV) − PPL_primary(FP16)`.\n\n### 8.4 Token-level PPL diagnostic\nFor each (W,A,KV) cell, also report decile-PPL over the evaluation token stream to detect localized degradation (long-tail tokens).\n\n---\n\n## 9. SQNR computation\n\nFor a signal `x` and its quantized-reconstructed signal `x_hat = dequant(quant(x))`, where residual noise `e = x − x_hat`:\n\n```\nSQNR (linear) = ‖x‖²_2 / ( ‖e‖²_2 + ε )        # ε = 1e-12 stability\nSQNR (dB)     = 10 · log10( SQNR_linear )\n```\n\n### 9.1 Where SQNR is computed\n- **Per-layer**, per signal kind:\n  - `sqnr_A_layer` — activations at the input of each `nn.Linear` (post-norm, pre-matmul) for every token in the window.\n  - `sqnr_W_layer` — weights, computed once per cell (since weights are pre-quantized, not swept).\n  - `sqnr_K_layer`, `sqnr_V_layer` — KV cache, per token per head per layer.\n- **Aggregated per cell** as geometric-mean across layers (because dB is already log):\n  `SQNR_W_cell = mean_layer SQNR_W_layer`, etc.\n  - Reported in **dB**.\n- The cell-level **mean-KV-SQNR** is reported separately:\n  `mean_kv_sqnr_cell = 0.5 · (SQNR_K_cell + SQNR_V_cell)`.\n\n### 9.2 SQNR per token\nFor `sqnr_A_per_token`, the per-token numerator uses ‖x_t‖² over the row and the residual uses ‖e_t‖². Reported as a tensor (B, T, n_layers) for at most 4 selected windows per cell to bound storage.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Analysis plan\n\n### 10.1 Optimal α extraction — per cell\nFor each `(b_w, b_a, b_k, b_v)` cell:\n1. Fit PPL(α) as a 1-D curve over the 19 α points.\n2. Estimate α\\*_cell = `argmin_α PPL_primary(α; cell)` on the discrete grid; bracket via parabolic interpolation between the three lowest PPL points to refine.\n3. Report `PPL\\*_cell = PPL(α\\*_cell)`, `ΔPPL\\*_cell`, `SQNR_A(α\\*_cell)`, and the U-shape's curvature `(PPL(0.1) − PPL(α\\*_cell)) / (PPL(1.0) − PPL(α\\*_cell))`.\n\n### 10.2 Optimal α — aggregated\n- Plot per-cell curves overlaid; compute aggregation only across (b_k, b_v) within a fixed (b_w, b_a) to test H3 (KV-orthogonality).\n- Report `α\\*_agg(W,A) = argmin_α  mean_{KV} ΔPPL(α; W,A,KV)` (and CB-aggregated PPL separately).\n- The \"**optimal fixed clipping ratio that balances quantization error and model accuracy**\" is reported as `α\\*_agg(W8A8)` (the highest-precision cell) since H3 predicts invariance and that cell is the most useful practical answer.\n\n### 10.3 Plots\n- PPL vs α at constant (b_w, b_a), 3 curves per plot (one per KV config).\n- ΔPPL vs α (same topology).\n- SQNR_A (dB) vs α.\n- PPL vs SQNR_A in the same cell (parametric in α) — explicit \"error vs accuracy\" scatter.\n- Layer-wise heatmap of `SQNR_A_layer` for cell (W4A4, K4V4) at α\\* — tests whether degradation is uniform across layers.\n\n### 10.4 Threshold-based \"acceptable α\" set\nDefine acceptable α region `A_ε(W,A,KV) = { α : ΔPPL(α) ≤ ε }`.\n- ε values stated in the design only as **target thresholds**, not as measured: `ε = 0.5` (W8A8), `ε = 1.0` (W4A8), `ε = 3.0` (W4A4). The thresholds are **post-hoc revision-safe**: final report repairs ε based on observed ΔPPL to keep acceptance intervals non-trivial (≥ 3 contiguous α points).\n- Report `α_safe_low(W,A,KV) = inf A_ε`, `α_safe_high(W,A,KV) = sup A_ε`.\n\n### 10.5 Statistical handling\n- 95% CI on ΔPPL and SQNR_A by **bootstrap over windows** with `B = 1000` resamples, seed = 0, block size = window.\n- PPL used in ΔPPL is the same scalar if it is a single-run measurement; CI applies to cell-level scopes only and is reported for cells at α ∈ {α\\*, α=0.7, α=1.0}.\n- Welch's two-sample t-test on ΔPPL between (W4A4) and (W4A8) at α\\* to report significance, **uncorrected p** (n ≤ 18 takes us out of asymptotic territory).\n\n### 10.6 Sensitivity analysis\n- S1 (per-layer α): compare global α\\* to per-layer α\\* profile via per-layer pareto curve.\n- S2 (per-tensor activations): compare ΔPPL(W\\*,A\\*,KV\\*,α\\*) vs (per-token) at identical bit widths.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Output schema\n\nCSV (or Parquet, schema-preserving) with one row per `(cell_id, alpha, kv_id)` measurement.\n\n```\ncell_id,                        # string e.g. \"W4A4-K4V4\"\nalpha,                          # float\nb_w, b_a, b_k, b_v,             # ints in {4,8}\nppl_primary,                    # float (exp of mean NLL)\nppl_secondary,                  # float (sliding-window variant)\ndelta_ppl,                      # float = ppl_primary − ppl_primary_baseline\nsqnr_W_db,                      # float\nsqnr_A_db,                      # float\nsqnr_K_db, sqnr_V_db,           # floats\nmean_kv_sqnr_db,                # float = 0.5 · (sqnr_K_db + sqnr_V_db)\nsqnr_A_per_token_dB_mean,       # float (per-token then averaged)\nn_windows,                      # int\nn_tokens,                       # int (n_windows · 2048)\nwallclock_seconds,              # int (host measurement)\nnotes,                          # free text (e.g. \"control\", \"sensitivity-s1\")\n```\n\nAuxiliary per-layer table (Parquet, joined on `cell_id, alpha, layer_idx`):\n```\ncell_id, alpha, layer_idx, sqnr_A_db, sqnr_W_db, sqnr_K_db, sqnr_V_db\n```\n\nAuxiliary per-token table (sparse, only for 4 selected windows per cell × ≥ 2 cells):\n```\ncell_id, alpha, window_idx, token_idx, sqnr_A_db\n```\n\n---\n\n## 12. Feasibility & runtime estimate\n\n### 12.1 Per-run cost on A100-80GB\n- LLaMA-2-7B FP16 forward cost ≈ 1 forward in ~50–80 ms with seq_len=2048 on A100 when using `torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention`.\n- One PPL eval pass over WT-2 test (≈ 140 windows × 2048 tokens) without quantization ≈ 7–10 s.\n- With custom Python runtime quant (no fused kernel): each `quant/dequant + matmul` adds overhead ≈ 30–60 ms per token ⇒ ~70–140 s per cell.\n  - Per-cell estimate (full α sweep at fixed cell): 19 α × 3 KV × 80 s ≈ **76 min / cell**.\n- Total full sweep (3 cells + overhead): ~3.8 hours + ~1 baseline + ~controls ≈ **5 hours** end-to-end.\n- With a more realistic 3–5× conservative constant (Python overhead dominates in custom quantization): **8–20 hours** on a single A100.\n\n### 12.2 Reduced-mode estimate\n- 6 α × 3 KV × 3 cells ≈ 54 cells × 80 s ≈ **72 min** sweep.\n- With realistic overhead: **3–6 hours**.\n\n### 12.3 Reduced-mode selection rule\nSwitch to reduced mode if **any** of the following:\n- (i) wall-clock budget ≤ 4 hours,\n- (ii) PPL gap between α=0.10 and α=0.30 is already > 50 in the first measured cell (U-shape clearly present, fine sampling not needed),\n- (iii) PPL min across the 6 reduced α is at a boundary (α=1.0 or α=0.30), indicating a denser sweep would help and we should increase density not breadth.\n\n### 12.4 Storage budget\n- Output CSV ≈ 200 rows × 1 KB ≈ 0.2 MB.\n- Per-layer table ≈ 200 × 32 × 0.1 KB ≈ 0.6 MB.\n- Per-token table (sparse) ≤ 5 MB.\n- Checkpoints for the patched model ≤ 50 MB.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Explicit assumptions list\n\n| id | assumption | source / why |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 | α is a **single global scalar** shared across all layers and tokens; per-layer α is a sensitivity variant only. | locked by researcher |\n| A2 | Eval dataset is **WikiText-2-raw-v1 test** split (≈ 287 247 tokens). | convention; not in /workspace |\n| A3 | `seq_len = 2048`. | locked by researcher |\n| A4 | PPL primary = **concatenated non-overlapping** window sum; secondary = pooled sliding-window (stride = seq_len). | locked by researcher |\n| A5 | (b_w, b_a) ∈ `{W4A4, W4A8, W8A8}` only. | locked by researcher |\n| A6 | KV scheme = **asymmetric per-token per-head-channel** (uint). | researcher-specified family |\n| A7 | KV is **re-quantized only when newly produced**; previously stored KV entries stay quantized. | standard sequential decoding |\n| A8 | Weights = **per-output-channel symmetric** int, pre-quantized once. | runtime-friendly, standard |\n| A9 | Activations = **per-token symmetric** int; α enters `c_t = α · max_i|x_{t,i}|`. | locked by researcher |\n| A10 | α = 1.0 is the **boundary reference** (no clipping) and also a sweep point. | locked by researcher |\n| A11 | Hardware target is **1× A100-80GB** with ≥ 30 GB free disk and ≥ 24 CPU cores. | research-task norm |\n| A12 | SQNR is computed in **dB**, geometric-mean (linear-mean in dB) across layers. | measurement of quantization error |\n| A13 | ε thresholds for \"acceptable α\" (`ε = 0.5/1.0/3.0`) are **post-hoc revisable** but stated as targets. | presentation pragmatism |\n| A14 | Eval uses **greedy LM-loss only**, no sampling. | measurement-isolation |\n| A15 | Tokenizer = HF `LlamaTokenizerFast`, BOS prepended once on the concatenated string. | HF-eval-harness standard |\n| A16 | Numerical format = FP16 for non-quantized paths and BF16 storage of weight scales; quantization math in INT32 accumulators. | numerical safety |\n| A17 | Code path: monkey-patched HF model, no fused kernel required. | feasibility (A100 has no built-in per-token-quant kernel) |\n| A18 | Sandbox `/workspace` (no GPU, no network) hosts the **design only**; execution lives on an A100 host. | bubblewrap note in `task.json` |\n\n---\n\n## 14. Limitations and threats to validity\n\n1. **Single-model generalizability (internal).** *Only LLaMA-2-7B.* Other 7B models (MPT, Falcon, Mistral-7B at the same parameter count) may exhibit different α\\* due to differing activation statistics (e.g., GELU vs SwiGLU saturation). Threat reduced by reporting layer-wise heatmaps so per-model patterns can be compared externally.\n2. **Single-dataset generalizability.** *Only WikiText-2-raw-v1.* Code, math, and C4 perplexity can shift α\\* because token-level distributions differ. Mitigation: secondary PPL (§8.2) checks arithmetic-mean equivalence; the U-shape hypothesis is testable independent of dataset.\n3. **Global-α optimum may be local.** Per-layer-α (S1) is run only as a sensitivity variant; if S1 shows materially lower PPL at every layer ≠ global α\\*, the global-α headline number should be re-stated as a *coordinate of* per-layer calibration, not as the ultimate optimum.\n4. **Granularity choice may dominate.** Per-tensor activations (S2) tests whether per-token granularity is doing the heavy lifting. If S2 markedly degrades PPL at constant α, the U-shape is partly an artifact of per-token granularity, not pure α.\n5. **Numerical drift from non-fused runtime.** Custom Python quant introduces overhead and rounding-mismatch vs an FP16 reference. Mitigation: C8 (FP16 reruns every 30 cells) and parity test on squares with `torch.allclose` at 1e-2 abs/rel tolerance before logging PPL.\n6. **KV quant re-use confounds α interpretation.** Old KV stays quantized; new KV is re-quantized. The α effect could entangle with the KV cache freshness if the model is run in streaming mode. Mitigation: deterministic eval with `seq_len = 2048` and all KV quantization done inlockstep with the input window, not with cross-window carry-over.\n7. **Outlier-only α and fixed calibration windows.** First few windows of WT-2 may dominate the empirical α\\* because the per-token absmax profile changes with input distribution. Mitigation: report PPL on the first 10 windows separately (diagnostic tail check).\n8. **ε threshold is a designer choice.** H2's \"α\\* moves toward 1.0 with fewer bits\" depends on ε. We state ε as a target but mark it post-hoc revisable; reviewers are warned that the \"optimal fixed α\" headline depends on the chosen ε.\n9. **Bubblewrap execution is impossible in this sandbox.** The design is host-only; no measurements exist. Anything that smells like a numeric result in this document is a **prediction under stated assumptions**, not an observation.\n10. **No GPU kernel-level fidelity.** With a fused quantized-attention kernel (e.g., FlashAttention-int4 variants), the SQNR / PPL trade-off will differ slightly. Our results are limited to a **python-level runtime quantization** implementation.\n11. **Possible issue: `mean NLL` exponent bias.** Concatenated PPL is biased low (~1e-3 rel) when the test split is shorter than a full epoch. We mitigate by reporting a fixed token window count × seq_len; if N_primary < 80 000 we mark the cell as `n_windows_warning`.\n12. **Aspirational scope warning.** The full sweep enumerates 190 runs under A100 hosting. If enactment time is shorter than §12.1 budget, the reduced-mode selection rule (§12.3) kicks in automatically.\n\n---\n\n### End of specification.\n\nThis document is the deliverable. Nothing in this specification has been run. The \"Researcher supplies quantitative ε/data\" path remains open via the assumptions table.\n", "evidence": "Evidence collected during inspection (reused, no new facts):\n- `ls -la /workspace` confirms a single file `task.json`; no code, configs, prior write-ups, datasets, or model artifacts exist in the repository.\n- `find / -name 'track.md'` returned no results, confirming the inline note in `task.json` (which states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\") is itself the entire source-of-truth for the bubblewrap constraint set.\n- `python3 --version` → 3.12.3; `pip3 --version` → 24.0; `nproc` → 24; `free -h` → 125 GiB; `df -h /workspace` → ~775 GB free; no GPU device present.\n- Per `task.json` environment block: network disabled, system Python only, no preinstalled deps, repository `/workspace`, datasets empty.\n- All quantitative values in this specification (cell counts, α grid, runtime estimates, ε thresholds) are framed as **predictions, design targets, or post-hoc revisable assumptions**, not as measured results — consistent with the design-only mandate of `task.json` and researcher instruction (do not execute, do not claim outcomes).\n- Hypotheses H1–H4 in §2.2 are explicitly framed as predictions grounded in quantization-error intuition, not as observed outcomes, per the researcher's request that hypotheses \"predict the relationship between α and PPL based on quantization-error intuition.\"", "assumptions": ["A1 — α is a single global scalar shared across all layers and tokens; per-layer α is a sensitivity variant only (locked by researcher).</item>\n<item>A2 — Eval dataset = WikiText-2-raw-v1 test split (≈ 287 247 tokens).</item>\n<item>A3 — seq_len = 2048.</item>\n<item>A4 — PPL primary = concatenated non-overlapping; secondary = sliding-window stride = seq_len.</item>\n<item>A5 — (b_w, b_a) ∈ {W4A4, W4A8, W8A8}.</item>\n<item>A6 — KV = per-token per-head-channel asymmetric uint.</item>\n<item>A7 — KV re-quantized only at the step it is produced; previously stored KV remains quantized.</item>\n<item>A8 — Weights = per-output-channel symmetric int, pre-quantized once.</item>\n<item>A9 — Activations = per-token symmetric int; c_t = α · max_i|x_{t,i}|; α=1.0 is no-clipping boundary.</item>\n<item>A10 — Hardware target = 1× A100-80GB; ≥ 30 GB disk, 24 CPUs; sandbox only hosts the design.</item>\n<item>A11 — SQNR reported in dB, layer-averaged.</item>\n<item>A12 — ε thresholds ({0.5, 1.0, 3.0}) are post-hoc revisable targets, not measured outcomes.</item>\n<item>A13 — Eval = greedy LM-loss only, no sampling; HF LlamaTokenizerFast with one BOS prepended.</item>\n<item>A14 — Numerical format = FP16 paths, BF16 scale storage, INT32 quant accumulators.</item>\n<item>A15 — Implementation = monkey-patched HF LlamaForCausalLM, no fused kernel required.</item>\n<item>A16 — Runtime estimates (5–20 h full sweep on A100) are predictions, not measurements.</item>\n<item>A17 — Wall-clock, kernel, and storage budgets are provided as estimates and revised post-hoc if execution time diverges.</item>\n<item>A18 — Hardware target = 1× A100-80GB; ≥ 30 GB disk, 24 CPUs; sandbox only hosts the design.</item>\n<item>A19 — The deliverable is the specification only; no execution occurs in the bubblewrap.</item>\n<item>A20 — The Kaggle-style reduced-mode switch (6 evenly spaced α) is the documented fallback driven by §12.3 triggers, not an opportunistic recommendation.</item>\n</parameter>"], "uncertainties": ["Whether the bubblewrap is the canonical execution environment or only authoring environment — A18/A19 taken from task.json's \"compute is not required\" inline note; the design is host-only.</item>\n<item>Whether the FP16 baseline should be re-run between cells for drift control (C8) or only at sweep midpoint; we chose every ~30 cells.</item>\n<item>Whether α*=cell reports should be arithmetic- or geometric-mean across KV; H3 predicts near-invariance but numerical noise could obscure it.</item>\n<item>Whether per-layer-α sensitivity (S1) reveals a meaningfully different optimum in 9 runs; if so the headline α* answer is partially revised.</item>\n</parameter>"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-7a209490-62ad-4f91-9778-87a3378217d7", "tool_call": {"id": "call-7a209490-62ad-4f91-9778-87a3378217d7", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final experiment design: clipping-ratio sweep for LLaMA-2-7B runtime quant\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design \\u2014 Effect of Clipping Ratio \\u03b1 on Perplexity under Runtime Symmetric-Input / Asymmetric-KV Quantization (LLaMA-2-7B)\\n\\n> **Status:** Design-only specification. No code is executed, no measurements fabricated, no outcome claimed. This document is the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Title\\n\\n**Sensitivity of LLaMA-2-7B Perplexity to the Activation Clipping Ratio \\u03b1 under Symmetric Per-Token Input Quantization and Asymmetric Per-Token Per-Head-Channel KV-Cache Quantization.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Research question & hypotheses\\n\\n### 2.1 Research question\\n*For LLaMA-2-7B, holding the quantization scheme (W: per-out-channel symmetric, A: per-token symmetric, KV: per-token per-head-channel asymmetric) and bit widths in {W4A4, W4A8, W8A8} \\u00d7 {K4V4, K4V8, K8V8} fixed, how does the global activation clipping ratio \\u03b1 \\u2208 (0, 1] affect end-to-end perplexity on WikiText-2-raw-v1, and what value of \\u03b1 minimizes perplexity per (W,A,KV) cell?*\\n\\n### 2.2 Hypotheses (predictions, not results)\\nH1 (U-shape). *For every non-degenerate (W,A,KV) cell, perplexity as a function of \\u03b1 is U-shaped:* high at \\u03b1 \\u2192 0 (severe clipping \\u2192 large distortion from deleted activations), decreasing toward a minimum at some \\u03b1\\\\* \\u2208 (0.5, 1.0), then mildly increasing back as \\u03b1 \\u2192 1.0 (no clipping \\u2192 scale s_t is too coarse relative to the bulk of the distribution, so low \\u03b1 already known \\u03b1\\\\* \\u2248 0.7\\u20130.9 from prior literature on symmetrical activation clipping.*\\n\\nH2 (Bits dependence). *The location of \\u03b1\\\\* moves closer to 1.0 as activation bits decrease* \\u2014 at A4 the optimal \\u03b1 is lower than at A8 because aggressive quant bins reward smaller dynamic range; at A8 the cost of clipping outweighs the quantization-step benefit sooner, so \\u03b1\\\\* tends higher.\\n\\nH3 (KV is mostly orthogonal to \\u03b1\\\\*). *\\u03b1\\\\* is determined primarily by activation statistics and is approximately invariant to KV bits once KV is asymmetric per-token per-head-channel; KV mainly shifts the curve vertically (\\u0394PPL floor) rather than horizontally (\\u03b1\\\\*).*\\n\\nH4 (SQNR peak co-locates with PPL minimum). *Per-token activation SQNR (dB) is maximized roughly at \\u03b1\\\\* because SQNR captures the trade-off between clipping error and quantization-step error; matching peaks at \\u03b1\\\\* support the U-shape inference.*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Prerequisites & environment\\n\\n### 3.1 Hardware\\n- **Primary target:** 1\\u00d7 NVIDIA A100-80GB (compute capability 8.0). 24 CPU cores available for data loading; KV flows through GPU.\\n- **Memory budget:** ~14 GB for FP16 weights of LLaMA-2-7B; with quantized weights pre-cast it fits comfortably; KV cache \\u00d7 (seq_len, n_layers, n_heads, head_dim) at W4A8/W8A8 fits within ~16 GB for seq_len = 2048.\\n- **Disk:** \\u2265 30 GB free for weights snapshot + datasets cache.\\n\\n### 3.2 Software (host environment, not the bubblewrap)\\n- Python 3.10+; `torch \\u2265 2.1` (CUDA build); `transformers \\u2265 4.40`; `datasets \\u2265 2.18`; `huggingface_hub`; `safetensors`; `numpy`; `pandas`; `pyarrow`.\\n- Inference path: HuggingFace `LlamaForCausalLM` with a custom **monkey-patch** layer that replaces `nn.Linear` forward and attention KV-store with the quantized runtime described in \\u00a75; HF remains the source of truth for tokenizer, RoPE, and config.\\n\\n### 3.3 Sandbox caveat (acknowledged, not a blocker)\\nThe current `/workspace` is a bubblewrap without GPU and without network, so neither weights nor pip dependencies can be obtained here. The deliverable is **the specification of what runs on the A100 host**; the sandbox is used only to inspect and author the design (consistent with the inline note in `task.json`).\\n\\n### 3.4 Seed & determinism\\n- `torch.manual_seed(0)`, `torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(0)`; cuDNN disabled (deterministic path for evaluation). Greedy eval (no sampling); LM-loss only. HF `transformers` eval utilities compute the same loss on the same input regardless of seed, so seed primarily affects any random tie-breaking in tie-breaker passes; results are reproducible up to BF16 vs FP16 numerics on the host.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Model and dataset specifics\\n\\n### 4.1 Model \\u2014 LLaMA-2-7B\\n- HuggingFace snapshot path supplied via env var `LLAMA2_7B_PATH` (ASSUMPTION default `/models/llama-2-7b-hf`).\\n- Loaded as `AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(LLAMA2_7B_PATH, torch_dtype=torch.float16)` (FP16 baseline reference) and `(\\u2026, torch_dtype=torch.float16)` with the patched quantized forward for swept cells.\\n- Tokenizer: `AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(LLAMA2_7B_PATH)`, `use_fast=True`, `bos_token` prepended on the full concatenated string before windowing (single global BOS).\\n- **Frozen weights**, no fine-tuning, no LoRA, no PEFT, no quantization-aware training.\\n\\n### 4.2 Dataset \\u2014 WikiText-2 raw (ASSUMPTION A2)\\n- HuggingFace `datasets.load_dataset(\\\"wikitext\\\", \\\"wikitext-2-raw-v1\\\", split=\\\"test\\\")`.\\n- Concatenate the test split into a single string with the HF-eval-harness convention: rows joined by `\\\\n\\\\n` so paragraph breaks are preserved. Append EOS between documents via `tokenizer.eos_token`.\\n- No chat template; no few-shot prompts; no system message.\\n\\n### 4.3 Tokenization & windowing\\n- Concatenate, tokenize once \\u2192 a single int64 vector of length N \\u2248 287 247 tokens (full WT-2 raw test split).\\n- **Primary PPL (\\u00a78.1):** N // `seq_len` non-overlapping windows of exactly `seq_len = 2048` tokens; full-window language-model loss accumulated.\\n- **Secondary PPL (\\u00a78.2):** sliding windows of `seq_len = 2048` with stride 2048 (i.e. same boundary, included only as a robustness cross-check; equivalent to primary when N is a multiple of seq_len).\\n\\n### 4.4 Sequence length and sample count\\n- `seq_len = 2048` (A3). \\n- Number of evaluation windows: N // 2048; number of evaluation tokens N_primary = (N // 2048) \\u00d7 2048.\\n- The clipped tail of N (N \\u2212 N_primary tokens) is **discarded** for primary PPL; it stays discarded for all cells to keep the comparison fair.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Quantization formulation\\n\\n### 5.1 Notation\\n- Weight tensor `W \\u2208 \\u211d^{d_out \\u00d7 d_in}`; activation row `x_t \\u2208 \\u211d^{1 \\u00d7 d_in}` (one token's pre-matmul input); KV head-row `k_{t,h}, v_{t,h} \\u2208 \\u211d^{1 \\u00d7 head_dim}`.\\n- Bit widths: `b_w`, `b_a`, `b_k`, `b_v` \\u2208 {4, 8}. Integer levels: int8 range `[\\u2212Q_a, Q_a]` with `Q_a = 2^(b_a \\u2212 1) \\u2212 1`; uint range `[0, Q_u]` with `Q_u = 2^b_a \\u2212 1`.\\n- Clip ratio: scalar \\u03b1 \\u2208 (0, 1], single global value (A1).\\n\\n### 5.2 W \\u2014 Per-output-channel symmetric integer (pre-quantized)\\nFor each output channel `c \\u2208 {1,\\u2026,d_out}`:\\n```\\nm_w[c]   = max_i  |W[c, i]|\\ns_w[c]   = m_w[c] / Q_a_w        (Q_a_w = 2^(b_w \\u2212 1) \\u2212 1)\\nW_q[c,i] = clamp(round(W[c,i] / s_w[c]), \\u2212Q_a_w, Q_a_w)\\nW_dequant[c,i] = W_q[c,i] \\u00b7 s_w[c]\\n```\\n- Quantization is performed **once before the sweep**, stored as `W_q (int8/int32)` and `s_w (fp16)`.\\n\\n### 5.3 A \\u2014 Per-token symmetric integer (runtime, swept \\u03b1)\\nFor each token row `x_t` entering a `nn.Linear`:\\n```\\nm_t    = max_i |x_t[i]|\\nc_t    = \\u03b1 \\u00b7 m_t                              # \\u03b1=1.0 \\u21d2 c_t=m_t (no clipping boundary)\\ns_t    = c_t / Q_a_a                          # Q_a_a = 2^(b_a \\u2212 1) \\u2212 1\\nx_clipped[t,i] = clamp(x_t[i], \\u2212c_t, +c_t)\\nx_q[t,i] = round(x_clipped[t,i] / s_t)\\nx_dequant[t,i] = x_q[t,i] \\u00b7 s_t              # used inside the matmul\\n```\\n- Applied **on the fly**; pseudo-static for stable tensors (e.g. attention inputs after residual add are treated as fresh tokens because the per-token max depends on the running sequence).\\n\\n### 5.4 KV \\u2014 Per-token per-head-channel asymmetric unsigned (runtime)\\nFor each token *t*, for each head *h*, the head-row is a contiguous slice of length `head_dim`:\\n```\\nk_min   = min_i k_{t,h}[i]\\nk_max   = max_i k_{t,h}[i]\\ns_k     = (k_max \\u2212 k_min) / max(Q_u_k, 1)    # Q_u_k = 2^b_k \\u2212 1\\nz_k     = round(\\u2212k_min / s_k)                # zero-point, integer\\nk_q[i]  = clamp(round(k_{t,h}[i] / s_k) + z_k, 0, Q_u_k)\\nk_dequant[i] = (k_q[i] \\u2212 z_k) \\u00b7 s_k\\n```\\n- Same formula for V \\u2192 `s_v`, `z_v`.\\n- Quantization is performed **per newly produced token** (re-quantization cadence per token, A7); previously stored KV entries remain in their quantized form and are dequantized when read by attention.\\n\\n### 5.5 Pseudo-code for the patched quantized forward (single transformer block)\\n\\n```\\nforward_one_block(x_t, layer_state, \\u03b1, b_w, b_a, b_k, b_v):\\n    # x_t: (B, T, D), T tokens, B=1 in eval\\n\\n    # --- Attention block ---\\n    # Norm + W_q, W_k, W_v matmuls \\u2192 use W_q + runtime act quant (A)\\n    x_norm = RMSNorm(x_t)\\n    q_per_tok, s_a_q = quant_act_per_token(x_norm @ W_q^T, b_a, \\u03b1)\\n    k_per_tok, s_a_k = quant_act_per_token(x_norm @ W_k^T, b_a, \\u03b1)\\n    v_per_tok, s_a_v = quant_act_per_token(x_norm @ W_v^T, b_a, \\u03b1)\\n\\n    # Re-quantize K and V to asymmetric uint KV-grid for cache storage\\n    K_signed = k_per_tok * s_a_k                      # dequant back to FP\\n    V_signed = v_per_tok * s_a_v\\n    for h in n_heads:\\n        for t in T_newly_added:\\n            k_q, s_k, z_k, m_k_w = quant_kv_per_token_per_head(K_signed[t, h, :], b_k)\\n            v_q, s_v, z_v, m_v_w = quant_kv_per_token_per_head(V_signed[t, h, :], b_v)\\n            layer_state.cache_k.append(k_q, s_k, z_k)\\n            layer_state.cache_v.append(v_q, s_v, z_v)\\n\\n    # Attention: Q stays FP16 (or per-token int), K/V read \\u2192 dequant to FP16\\n    K_full = dequant_kv_cache(layer_state.cache_k)     # (B, T_total, n_heads, head_dim)\\n    V_full = dequant_kv_cache(layer_state.cache_v)\\n    attn_out = scaled_dot_product_attention(q_per_tok, K_full, V_full)\\n\\n    # --- Output projection ---\\n    attn_norm = RMSNorm(attn_out + x_t)                # residual outside quant scope\\n    a_in_q, s_a_o = quant_act_per_token(attn_norm, b_a, \\u03b1)\\n    out = (a_in_q * s_a_o) @ W_o_dequant              # W_o is per-out-channel symmetric\\n\\n    # --- FFN ---\\n    h1 = (quant_act_per_token(RMSNorm(out), b_a, \\u03b1)) @ W_1_dequant\\n    h1_act = swiglu(h1)\\n    h2 = (quant_act_per_token(h1_act, b_a, \\u03b1)) @ W_2_dequant\\n    return x_t + out + h2, layer_state\\n```\\n\\nNumbers logged per block (to compute SQNR, \\u00a79):\\n- `m_c_t_w = max_t (max_i |W_w[i]| / s_w[c] \\u2212 dequant(W_q))` (weight quantization noise)\\n- Residual quantization noise `e_a[t] = x_t \\u2212 dequant(quant(x_t))` per token\\n- KV quantization noise `e_k`, `e_v` per token per head.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Sweep design\\n\\n### 6.1 Sweep axes\\n\\n| axis | values | role |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| \\u03b1 (clip ratio, activations) | `{0.10, 0.20, 0.30, \\u2026, 0.95, 1.00}` (18 points) + orientation `\\u03b1 \\u2248 0.693` (mean-field 3\\u03c3 for a Gaussian) | primary swept axis |\\n| Weight bits `b_w` | {4, 8} | swept |\\n| Activation bits `b_a` | {4, 8} | swept (constraint `b_a \\u2265 4`; b_w=4 only combined with b_a=4) |\\n| KV bits `b_k` \\u00d7 `b_v` | {K4V4, K4V8, K8V8} | swept per (b_w, b_a) cell |\\n| Activation granularity | per-token, symmetric | fixed |\\n| Weight granularity | per-output-channel, symmetric | fixed |\\n| KV granularity | per-token per-head-channel, asymmetric | fixed |\\n| Eval dataset | WikiText-2-raw-v1 test | fixed |\\n| `seq_len` | 2048 | fixed |\\n| Prompt construct | concat + BOS, no chat template, LM-loss on all tokens | fixed |\\n| Baseline | FP16, no quant | fixed reference |\\n| \\u03b1 granularity | single global scalar across layers (A1) | fixed |\\n\\n### 6.2 (W,A) cells\\nEffective cells (per researcher constraint): `{W4A4, W4A8, W8A8}` \\u2014 3 cells.\\n\\n### 6.3 Cell count \\u2014 full sweep\\nPer cell: 19 \\u03b1 \\u00d7 3 KV configs = 57 measured runs.\\nAcross cells: 3 \\u00d7 57 = **171 measured runs**, plus:\\n- 1 \\u00d7 FP16 baseline\\n- Control ablation \\u03b1=1.0 (boundary reference) \\u2014 already in the 171; reused\\n- Control ablation \\u03b1\\u21920 \\u2014 already in the 171 (use \\u03b1 = 0.10)\\n- Sensitivity variant S1 (per-layer \\u03b1 instead of global): 1 representative \\u03b1\\\\*-region value \\u00d7 3 cells \\u00d7 3 KV = 9 runs\\n- Sensitivity variant S2 (per-tensor activations vs per-token): 1 representative \\u03b1-value \\u00d7 3 cells \\u00d7 3 KV = 9 runs\\n- Per-layer-\\u03b1 sweep shortcut (if time): 3 cells \\u00d7 3 KV \\u00d7 3 \\u03b1 = 27 runs\\n\\n**Total target \\u2248 171 + 1 + 9 + 9 = 190 runs** (without shortcut).\\n\\n### 6.4 Reduced-mode fallback (when compute is tight)\\n- Reduce \\u03b1 grid to 6 evenly spaced values: `{0.30, 0.45, 0.60, 0.75, 0.90, 1.00}`.\\n- Reduced cell count: 3 \\u00d7 6 \\u00d7 3 = **54 measured runs**, plus 1 FP16 + 9 + 9 = **73 runs**.\\n- Mandatory anchor: always include \\u03b1 \\u2208 {0.30, 0.45, 0.60, 0.75, 0.90, 1.00} and at least K4V4 in every cell so the U-shape can be verified.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Controls\\n\\n| control | purpose | details |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| C0 \\u2014 FP16 baseline | reference for \\u0394PPL | identical tokenizer, prompt, seq_len, eval dataset |\\n| C1 \\u2014 \\u03b1 = 1.0 (boundary) | shows effect of disabling clipping | \\u03b1 = 1.00 is itself a sweep point; reinforces H1 |\\n| C2 \\u2014 \\u03b1 \\u2192 0 (extreme clipping) | shows failure mode | \\u03b1 = 0.10; PPL expected to explode, anchors the U-shape's left arm |\\n| C3 \\u2014 per-layer \\u03b1 (sensitivity) | test whether global \\u03b1 misses a better profile | run with `(\\u03b1_l)` per-layer grid derived from absmax calibration (1 epoch) |\\n| C4 \\u2014 per-tensor activations (granularity sensitivity) | test whether per-token assumption is load-bearing | quantize per-tensor symmetric for activations only; everything else unchanged; representative run at one mid-\\u03b1 per cell |\\n| C5 \\u2014 K8V8 vs K4V8 (asymmetric sensitivity) | check whether asymmetric K/V width matters more than \\u03b1 | included automatically by sweeping (b_k, b_v) |\\n| C6 \\u2014 KV-bits-fixed variants | test orthogonality hypothesis (H3) | the full sweep already covers; no extra run |\\n| C7 \\u2014 orientation \\u03b1 \\u2248 0.693 (Gaussian 3\\u03c3 rule) | predictor vs measurement check | 1 per (cell, KV) = 9 runs |\\n| C8 \\u2014 duplicated FP16 baseline at sweep midpoint | numerical drift check | re-run FP16 every ~30 cells to flag BF16/FP16 drift |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Perplexity metric definition\\n\\n### 8.1 Primary \\u2014 Concatenated non-overlapping PPL\\n- Build a concatenated int64 token stream of length N.\\n- Compute P(N_primary // seq_len) windows of length seq_len = 2048, no overlap; the first seq_len tokens feed each window.\\n- For window `w`, HF-style shifted LM loss: `\\u2113_w = mean_{t\\u2208[1, seq_len)}  \\u2212log p_\\u03b8(x_w[t+1] | x_w[\\u2264t])`.\\n- **Primary:**\\n  `PPL_primary = exp( (\\u03a3_w \\u03a3_t \\u2113_w) / N_primary )`.\\n\\n### 8.2 Secondary \\u2014 Sliding-window PPL (stride = seq_len)\\n- Same non-overlapping partition as primary (since stride = seq_len), but computed as `PPL_windowed = exp( mean_w \\u2113_w )`.\\n- Acts as an arithmetic-mean variant to guard against pathological window-weighting in \\u00a78.1.\\n\\n### 8.3 \\u0394PPL (always reported per cell)\\n`\\u0394PPL(\\u03b1; W,A,KV) = PPL_primary(\\u03b1; W,A,KV) \\u2212 PPL_primary(FP16)`.\\n\\n### 8.4 Token-level PPL diagnostic\\nFor each (W,A,KV) cell, also report decile-PPL over the evaluation token stream to detect localized degradation (long-tail tokens).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. SQNR computation\\n\\nFor a signal `x` and its quantized-reconstructed signal `x_hat = dequant(quant(x))`, where residual noise `e = x \\u2212 x_hat`:\\n\\n```\\nSQNR (linear) = \\u2016x\\u2016\\u00b2_2 / ( \\u2016e\\u2016\\u00b2_2 + \\u03b5 )        # \\u03b5 = 1e-12 stability\\nSQNR (dB)     = 10 \\u00b7 log10( SQNR_linear )\\n```\\n\\n### 9.1 Where SQNR is computed\\n- **Per-layer**, per signal kind:\\n  - `sqnr_A_layer` \\u2014 activations at the input of each `nn.Linear` (post-norm, pre-matmul) for every token in the window.\\n  - `sqnr_W_layer` \\u2014 weights, computed once per cell (since weights are pre-quantized, not swept).\\n  - `sqnr_K_layer`, `sqnr_V_layer` \\u2014 KV cache, per token per head per layer.\\n- **Aggregated per cell** as geometric-mean across layers (because dB is already log):\\n  `SQNR_W_cell = mean_layer SQNR_W_layer`, etc.\\n  - Reported in **dB**.\\n- The cell-level **mean-KV-SQNR** is reported separately:\\n  `mean_kv_sqnr_cell = 0.5 \\u00b7 (SQNR_K_cell + SQNR_V_cell)`.\\n\\n### 9.2 SQNR per token\\nFor `sqnr_A_per_token`, the per-token numerator uses \\u2016x_t\\u2016\\u00b2 over the row and the residual uses \\u2016e_t\\u2016\\u00b2. Reported as a tensor (B, T, n_layers) for at most 4 selected windows per cell to bound storage.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Analysis plan\\n\\n### 10.1 Optimal \\u03b1 extraction \\u2014 per cell\\nFor each `(b_w, b_a, b_k, b_v)` cell:\\n1. Fit PPL(\\u03b1) as a 1-D curve over the 19 \\u03b1 points.\\n2. Estimate \\u03b1\\\\*_cell = `argmin_\\u03b1 PPL_primary(\\u03b1; cell)` on the discrete grid; bracket via parabolic interpolation between the three lowest PPL points to refine.\\n3. Report `PPL\\\\*_cell = PPL(\\u03b1\\\\*_cell)`, `\\u0394PPL\\\\*_cell`, `SQNR_A(\\u03b1\\\\*_cell)`, and the U-shape's curvature `(PPL(0.1) \\u2212 PPL(\\u03b1\\\\*_cell)) / (PPL(1.0) \\u2212 PPL(\\u03b1\\\\*_cell))`.\\n\\n### 10.2 Optimal \\u03b1 \\u2014 aggregated\\n- Plot per-cell curves overlaid; compute aggregation only across (b_k, b_v) within a fixed (b_w, b_a) to test H3 (KV-orthogonality).\\n- Report `\\u03b1\\\\*_agg(W,A) = argmin_\\u03b1  mean_{KV} \\u0394PPL(\\u03b1; W,A,KV)` (and CB-aggregated PPL separately).\\n- The \\\"**optimal fixed clipping ratio that balances quantization error and model accuracy**\\\" is reported as `\\u03b1\\\\*_agg(W8A8)` (the highest-precision cell) since H3 predicts invariance and that cell is the most useful practical answer.\\n\\n### 10.3 Plots\\n- PPL vs \\u03b1 at constant (b_w, b_a), 3 curves per plot (one per KV config).\\n- \\u0394PPL vs \\u03b1 (same topology).\\n- SQNR_A (dB) vs \\u03b1.\\n- PPL vs SQNR_A in the same cell (parametric in \\u03b1) \\u2014 explicit \\\"error vs accuracy\\\" scatter.\\n- Layer-wise heatmap of `SQNR_A_layer` for cell (W4A4, K4V4) at \\u03b1\\\\* \\u2014 tests whether degradation is uniform across layers.\\n\\n### 10.4 Threshold-based \\\"acceptable \\u03b1\\\" set\\nDefine acceptable \\u03b1 region `A_\\u03b5(W,A,KV) = { \\u03b1 : \\u0394PPL(\\u03b1) \\u2264 \\u03b5 }`.\\n- \\u03b5 values stated in the design only as **target thresholds**, not as measured: `\\u03b5 = 0.5` (W8A8), `\\u03b5 = 1.0` (W4A8), `\\u03b5 = 3.0` (W4A4). The thresholds are **post-hoc revision-safe**: final report repairs \\u03b5 based on observed \\u0394PPL to keep acceptance intervals non-trivial (\\u2265 3 contiguous \\u03b1 points).\\n- Report `\\u03b1_safe_low(W,A,KV) = inf A_\\u03b5`, `\\u03b1_safe_high(W,A,KV) = sup A_\\u03b5`.\\n\\n### 10.5 Statistical handling\\n- 95% CI on \\u0394PPL and SQNR_A by **bootstrap over windows** with `B = 1000` resamples, seed = 0, block size = window.\\n- PPL used in \\u0394PPL is the same scalar if it is a single-run measurement; CI applies to cell-level scopes only and is reported for cells at \\u03b1 \\u2208 {\\u03b1\\\\*, \\u03b1=0.7, \\u03b1=1.0}.\\n- Welch's two-sample t-test on \\u0394PPL between (W4A4) and (W4A8) at \\u03b1\\\\* to report significance, **uncorrected p** (n \\u2264 18 takes us out of asymptotic territory).\\n\\n### 10.6 Sensitivity analysis\\n- S1 (per-layer \\u03b1): compare global \\u03b1\\\\* to per-layer \\u03b1\\\\* profile via per-layer pareto curve.\\n- S2 (per-tensor activations): compare \\u0394PPL(W\\\\*,A\\\\*,KV\\\\*,\\u03b1\\\\*) vs (per-token) at identical bit widths.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Output schema\\n\\nCSV (or Parquet, schema-preserving) with one row per `(cell_id, alpha, kv_id)` measurement.\\n\\n```\\ncell_id,                        # string e.g. \\\"W4A4-K4V4\\\"\\nalpha,                          # float\\nb_w, b_a, b_k, b_v,             # ints in {4,8}\\nppl_primary,                    # float (exp of mean NLL)\\nppl_secondary,                  # float (sliding-window variant)\\ndelta_ppl,                      # float = ppl_primary \\u2212 ppl_primary_baseline\\nsqnr_W_db,                      # float\\nsqnr_A_db,                      # float\\nsqnr_K_db, sqnr_V_db,           # floats\\nmean_kv_sqnr_db,                # float = 0.5 \\u00b7 (sqnr_K_db + sqnr_V_db)\\nsqnr_A_per_token_dB_mean,       # float (per-token then averaged)\\nn_windows,                      # int\\nn_tokens,                       # int (n_windows \\u00b7 2048)\\nwallclock_seconds,              # int (host measurement)\\nnotes,                          # free text (e.g. \\\"control\\\", \\\"sensitivity-s1\\\")\\n```\\n\\nAuxiliary per-layer table (Parquet, joined on `cell_id, alpha, layer_idx`):\\n```\\ncell_id, alpha, layer_idx, sqnr_A_db, sqnr_W_db, sqnr_K_db, sqnr_V_db\\n```\\n\\nAuxiliary per-token table (sparse, only for 4 selected windows per cell \\u00d7 \\u2265 2 cells):\\n```\\ncell_id, alpha, window_idx, token_idx, sqnr_A_db\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Feasibility & runtime estimate\\n\\n### 12.1 Per-run cost on A100-80GB\\n- LLaMA-2-7B FP16 forward cost \\u2248 1 forward in ~50\\u201380 ms with seq_len=2048 on A100 when using `torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention`.\\n- One PPL eval pass over WT-2 test (\\u2248 140 windows \\u00d7 2048 tokens) without quantization \\u2248 7\\u201310 s.\\n- With custom Python runtime quant (no fused kernel): each `quant/dequant + matmul` adds overhead \\u2248 30\\u201360 ms per token \\u21d2 ~70\\u2013140 s per cell.\\n  - Per-cell estimate (full \\u03b1 sweep at fixed cell): 19 \\u03b1 \\u00d7 3 KV \\u00d7 80 s \\u2248 **76 min / cell**.\\n- Total full sweep (3 cells + overhead): ~3.8 hours + ~1 baseline + ~controls \\u2248 **5 hours** end-to-end.\\n- With a more realistic 3\\u20135\\u00d7 conservative constant (Python overhead dominates in custom quantization): **8\\u201320 hours** on a single A100.\\n\\n### 12.2 Reduced-mode estimate\\n- 6 \\u03b1 \\u00d7 3 KV \\u00d7 3 cells \\u2248 54 cells \\u00d7 80 s \\u2248 **72 min** sweep.\\n- With realistic overhead: **3\\u20136 hours**.\\n\\n### 12.3 Reduced-mode selection rule\\nSwitch to reduced mode if **any** of the following:\\n- (i) wall-clock budget \\u2264 4 hours,\\n- (ii) PPL gap between \\u03b1=0.10 and \\u03b1=0.30 is already > 50 in the first measured cell (U-shape clearly present, fine sampling not needed),\\n- (iii) PPL min across the 6 reduced \\u03b1 is at a boundary (\\u03b1=1.0 or \\u03b1=0.30), indicating a denser sweep would help and we should increase density not breadth.\\n\\n### 12.4 Storage budget\\n- Output CSV \\u2248 200 rows \\u00d7 1 KB \\u2248 0.2 MB.\\n- Per-layer table \\u2248 200 \\u00d7 32 \\u00d7 0.1 KB \\u2248 0.6 MB.\\n- Per-token table (sparse) \\u2264 5 MB.\\n- Checkpoints for the patched model \\u2264 50 MB.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Explicit assumptions list\\n\\n| id | assumption | source / why |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A1 | \\u03b1 is a **single global scalar** shared across all layers and tokens; per-layer \\u03b1 is a sensitivity variant only. | locked by researcher |\\n| A2 | Eval dataset is **WikiText-2-raw-v1 test** split (\\u2248 287 247 tokens). | convention; not in /workspace |\\n| A3 | `seq_len = 2048`. | locked by researcher |\\n| A4 | PPL primary = **concatenated non-overlapping** window sum; secondary = pooled sliding-window (stride = seq_len). | locked by researcher |\\n| A5 | (b_w, b_a) \\u2208 `{W4A4, W4A8, W8A8}` only. | locked by researcher |\\n| A6 | KV scheme = **asymmetric per-token per-head-channel** (uint). | researcher-specified family |\\n| A7 | KV is **re-quantized only when newly produced**; previously stored KV entries stay quantized. | standard sequential decoding |\\n| A8 | Weights = **per-output-channel symmetric** int, pre-quantized once. | runtime-friendly, standard |\\n| A9 | Activations = **per-token symmetric** int; \\u03b1 enters `c_t = \\u03b1 \\u00b7 max_i|x_{t,i}|`. | locked by researcher |\\n| A10 | \\u03b1 = 1.0 is the **boundary reference** (no clipping) and also a sweep point. | locked by researcher |\\n| A11 | Hardware target is **1\\u00d7 A100-80GB** with \\u2265 30 GB free disk and \\u2265 24 CPU cores. | research-task norm |\\n| A12 | SQNR is computed in **dB**, geometric-mean (linear-mean in dB) across layers. | measurement of quantization error |\\n| A13 | \\u03b5 thresholds for \\\"acceptable \\u03b1\\\" (`\\u03b5 = 0.5/1.0/3.0`) are **post-hoc revisable** but stated as targets. | presentation pragmatism |\\n| A14 | Eval uses **greedy LM-loss only**, no sampling. | measurement-isolation |\\n| A15 | Tokenizer = HF `LlamaTokenizerFast`, BOS prepended once on the concatenated string. | HF-eval-harness standard |\\n| A16 | Numerical format = FP16 for non-quantized paths and BF16 storage of weight scales; quantization math in INT32 accumulators. | numerical safety |\\n| A17 | Code path: monkey-patched HF model, no fused kernel required. | feasibility (A100 has no built-in per-token-quant kernel) |\\n| A18 | Sandbox `/workspace` (no GPU, no network) hosts the **design only**; execution lives on an A100 host. | bubblewrap note in `task.json` |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 14. Limitations and threats to validity\\n\\n1. **Single-model generalizability (internal).** *Only LLaMA-2-7B.* Other 7B models (MPT, Falcon, Mistral-7B at the same parameter count) may exhibit different \\u03b1\\\\* due to differing activation statistics (e.g., GELU vs SwiGLU saturation). Threat reduced by reporting layer-wise heatmaps so per-model patterns can be compared externally.\\n2. **Single-dataset generalizability.** *Only WikiText-2-raw-v1.* Code, math, and C4 perplexity can shift \\u03b1\\\\* because token-level distributions differ. Mitigation: secondary PPL (\\u00a78.2) checks arithmetic-mean equivalence; the U-shape hypothesis is testable independent of dataset.\\n3. **Global-\\u03b1 optimum may be local.** Per-layer-\\u03b1 (S1) is run only as a sensitivity variant; if S1 shows materially lower PPL at every layer \\u2260 global \\u03b1\\\\*, the global-\\u03b1 headline number should be re-stated as a *coordinate of* per-layer calibration, not as the ultimate optimum.\\n4. **Granularity choice may dominate.** Per-tensor activations (S2) tests whether per-token granularity is doing the heavy lifting. If S2 markedly degrades PPL at constant \\u03b1, the U-shape is partly an artifact of per-token granularity, not pure \\u03b1.\\n5. **Numerical drift from non-fused runtime.** Custom Python quant introduces overhead and rounding-mismatch vs an FP16 reference. Mitigation: C8 (FP16 reruns every 30 cells) and parity test on squares with `torch.allclose` at 1e-2 abs/rel tolerance before logging PPL.\\n6. **KV quant re-use confounds \\u03b1 interpretation.** Old KV stays quantized; new KV is re-quantized. The \\u03b1 effect could entangle with the KV cache freshness if the model is run in streaming mode. Mitigation: deterministic eval with `seq_len = 2048` and all KV quantization done inlockstep with the input window, not with cross-window carry-over.\\n7. **Outlier-only \\u03b1 and fixed calibration windows.** First few windows of WT-2 may dominate the empirical \\u03b1\\\\* because the per-token absmax profile changes with input distribution. Mitigation: report PPL on the first 10 windows separately (diagnostic tail check).\\n8. **\\u03b5 threshold is a designer choice.** H2's \\\"\\u03b1\\\\* moves toward 1.0 with fewer bits\\\" depends on \\u03b5. We state \\u03b5 as a target but mark it post-hoc revisable; reviewers are warned that the \\\"optimal fixed \\u03b1\\\" headline depends on the chosen \\u03b5.\\n9. **Bubblewrap execution is impossible in this sandbox.** The design is host-only; no measurements exist. Anything that smells like a numeric result in this document is a **prediction under stated assumptions**, not an observation.\\n10. **No GPU kernel-level fidelity.** With a fused quantized-attention kernel (e.g., FlashAttention-int4 variants), the SQNR / PPL trade-off will differ slightly. Our results are limited to a **python-level runtime quantization** implementation.\\n11. **Possible issue: `mean NLL` exponent bias.** Concatenated PPL is biased low (~1e-3 rel) when the test split is shorter than a full epoch. We mitigate by reporting a fixed token window count \\u00d7 seq_len; if N_primary < 80 000 we mark the cell as `n_windows_warning`.\\n12. **Aspirational scope warning.** The full sweep enumerates 190 runs under A100 hosting. If enactment time is shorter than \\u00a712.1 budget, the reduced-mode selection rule (\\u00a712.3) kicks in automatically.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### End of specification.\\n\\nThis document is the deliverable. Nothing in this specification has been run. The \\\"Researcher supplies quantitative \\u03b5/data\\\" path remains open via the assumptions table.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"Evidence collected during inspection (reused, no new facts):\\n- `ls -la /workspace` confirms a single file `task.json`; no code, configs, prior write-ups, datasets, or model artifacts exist in the repository.\\n- `find / -name 'track.md'` returned no results, confirming the inline note in `task.json` (which states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\") is itself the entire source-of-truth for the bubblewrap constraint set.\\n- `python3 --version` \\u2192 3.12.3; `pip3 --version` \\u2192 24.0; `nproc` \\u2192 24; `free -h` \\u2192 125 GiB; `df -h /workspace` \\u2192 ~775 GB free; no GPU device present.\\n- Per `task.json` environment block: network disabled, system Python only, no preinstalled deps, repository `/workspace`, datasets empty.\\n- All quantitative values in this specification (cell counts, \\u03b1 grid, runtime estimates, \\u03b5 thresholds) are framed as **predictions, design targets, or post-hoc revisable assumptions**, not as measured results \\u2014 consistent with the design-only mandate of `task.json` and researcher instruction (do not execute, do not claim outcomes).\\n- Hypotheses H1\\u2013H4 in \\u00a72.2 are explicitly framed as predictions grounded in quantization-error intuition, not as observed outcomes, per the researcher's request that hypotheses \\\"predict the relationship between \\u03b1 and PPL based on quantization-error intuition.\\\"\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1 \\u2014 \\u03b1 is a single global scalar shared across all layers and tokens; per-layer \\u03b1 is a sensitivity variant only (locked by researcher).</item>\\n<item>A2 \\u2014 Eval dataset = WikiText-2-raw-v1 test split (\\u2248 287 247 tokens).</item>\\n<item>A3 \\u2014 seq_len = 2048.</item>\\n<item>A4 \\u2014 PPL primary = concatenated non-overlapping; secondary = sliding-window stride = seq_len.</item>\\n<item>A5 \\u2014 (b_w, b_a) \\u2208 {W4A4, W4A8, W8A8}.</item>\\n<item>A6 \\u2014 KV = per-token per-head-channel asymmetric uint.</item>\\n<item>A7 \\u2014 KV re-quantized only at the step it is produced; previously stored KV remains quantized.</item>\\n<item>A8 \\u2014 Weights = per-output-channel symmetric int, pre-quantized once.</item>\\n<item>A9 \\u2014 Activations = per-token symmetric int; c_t = \\u03b1 \\u00b7 max_i|x_{t,i}|; \\u03b1=1.0 is no-clipping boundary.</item>\\n<item>A10 \\u2014 Hardware target = 1\\u00d7 A100-80GB; \\u2265 30 GB disk, 24 CPUs; sandbox only hosts the design.</item>\\n<item>A11 \\u2014 SQNR reported in dB, layer-averaged.</item>\\n<item>A12 \\u2014 \\u03b5 thresholds ({0.5, 1.0, 3.0}) are post-hoc revisable targets, not measured outcomes.</item>\\n<item>A13 \\u2014 Eval = greedy LM-loss only, no sampling; HF LlamaTokenizerFast with one BOS prepended.</item>\\n<item>A14 \\u2014 Numerical format = FP16 paths, BF16 scale storage, INT32 quant accumulators.</item>\\n<item>A15 \\u2014 Implementation = monkey-patched HF LlamaForCausalLM, no fused kernel required.</item>\\n<item>A16 \\u2014 Runtime estimates (5\\u201320 h full sweep on A100) are predictions, not measurements.</item>\\n<item>A17 \\u2014 Wall-clock, kernel, and storage budgets are provided as estimates and revised post-hoc if execution time diverges.</item>\\n<item>A18 \\u2014 Hardware target = 1\\u00d7 A100-80GB; \\u2265 30 GB disk, 24 CPUs; sandbox only hosts the design.</item>\\n<item>A19 \\u2014 The deliverable is the specification only; no execution occurs in the bubblewrap.</item>\\n<item>A20 \\u2014 The Kaggle-style reduced-mode switch (6 evenly spaced \\u03b1) is the documented fallback driven by \\u00a712.3 triggers, not an opportunistic recommendation.</item>\\n</parameter>\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether the bubblewrap is the canonical execution environment or only authoring environment \\u2014 A18/A19 taken from task.json's \\\"compute is not required\\\" inline note; the design is host-only.</item>\\n<item>Whether the FP16 baseline should be re-run between cells for drift control (C8) or only at sweep midpoint; we chose every ~30 cells.</item>\\n<item>Whether \\u03b1*=cell reports should be arithmetic- or geometric-mean across KV; H3 predicts near-invariance but numerical noise could obscure it.</item>\\n<item>Whether per-layer-\\u03b1 sensitivity (S1) reveals a meaningfully different optimum in 9 runs; if so the headline \\u03b1* answer is partially revised.</item>\\n</parameter>\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-0d5ea803-ab31-4f14-82b4-116dae051022", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
